diagnosis_text:
A thorough sweep of every packet-drop mechanism in this two-router BGP topology found no fault. End-to-end reachability between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) is fully functional: multiple ping runs in both directions at both regular and flood rates produced 0% packet loss with sub-1 ms RTT. The BGP session between AS 1 (router1) and AS 2 (router2) has been continuously established for the entire observation window with correct prefixes installed on both sides, no Notifications, and normal 180 s hold / 60 s keepalive timers. There are no iptables or nftables forwarding rules, no netem or tc-based drop disciplines, no interface errors, and no rp_filter asymmetry. IPv4 forwarding is enabled on both routers. The only non-standard configuration item found is the presence of `debug bgp keepalives`, `debug bgp updates in`, and `debug bgp updates out` in both routers' `frr.conf` and running config. These are verbose-logging flags only; because the Linux kernel's FIB handles all packet forwarding independently of the FRR process, they cannot cause packet loss. The generated log traffic (one keepalive pair per 60 s) is negligible — the log file was 49 lines and 8 KB after several minutes of operation. The reported symptom — approximately 33% ping loss to the partner host — cannot be reproduced under any test condition and has no corresponding drop evidence anywhere in the data plane. The network is operating correctly; no corrective action is required. As a hygiene measure, the unnecessary `debug bgp` statements may be removed from both routers' configs to avoid clutter.
=== END ===